1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:03,300
It is the ultimate survival school

2
00:00:03,400 --> 00:00:06,300
where soldiers learn to kill or be killed.

3
00:00:06,400 --> 00:00:10,340
They are 21st century warriors preparing both their bodies

4
00:00:10,440 --> 00:00:12,440
and their minds for battle.

5
00:00:12,540 --> 00:00:15,840
Now, combat training on Modern Marvels.

6
00:00:42,040 --> 00:00:45,040
Oh, oh, ah!

7
00:00:45,140 --> 00:00:46,040
Oh, hit him!

8
00:00:46,140 --> 00:00:47,040
Hit him!

9
00:00:47,140 --> 00:00:48,040
You don't want to bite him!

10
00:00:48,140 --> 00:00:49,040
Hit him!

11
00:00:49,140 --> 00:00:50,040
Hit him!

12
00:00:50,140 --> 00:00:52,880
In the US regular forces, nothing compares

13
00:00:52,980 --> 00:00:57,580
to the physical training that a Marine has to go through.

14
00:00:57,680 --> 00:01:00,200
These recruits are fiercely attacking each other at close

15
00:01:00,300 --> 00:01:03,100
quarters with heavily padded pugil sticks.

16
00:01:03,200 --> 00:01:06,600
The red end represents a bayonet, the black end,

17
00:01:06,700 --> 00:01:08,300
the butt of a rifle.

18
00:01:08,400 --> 00:01:09,800
Don't just lay a stick on him, hit him!

19
00:01:09,801 --> 00:01:13,640
At this level of combat, at the individual level of combat,

20
00:01:13,641 --> 00:01:18,200
it is desperate eye to eye, man to man, you or me, live or die.

21
00:01:23,720 --> 00:01:25,120
There's no second place in combat,

22
00:01:25,121 --> 00:01:27,440
and that's kind of what I try to portray to him here,

23
00:01:27,441 --> 00:01:29,360
is that no matter how the odds are stacked against you,

24
00:01:29,361 --> 00:01:34,760
you still have to put out 100% effort or you're not coming home.

25
00:01:34,761 --> 00:01:35,760
Run!

26
00:01:35,761 --> 00:01:38,320
What are you waiting for?

27
00:01:38,321 --> 00:01:39,220
Huzzah!

28
00:01:39,221 --> 00:01:40,420
There's more war to fight!

29
00:01:40,421 --> 00:01:41,420
Huzzah!

30
00:01:41,421 --> 00:01:42,420
Huzzah!

31
00:01:42,421 --> 00:01:46,420
Combat training has but one simple goal,

32
00:01:46,421 --> 00:01:49,740
to take a civilian with no military skills whatsoever

33
00:01:49,741 --> 00:01:52,800
and turn him into a physically fit, psychologically tough

34
00:01:52,801 --> 00:01:56,040
soldier who is familiar with the tools of war

35
00:01:56,041 --> 00:01:57,880
and is willing to use them.

36
00:01:57,881 --> 00:01:58,880
Let's go, butt-struck!

37
00:01:58,881 --> 00:01:59,880
Faster, harder!

38
00:01:59,881 --> 00:02:01,780
All the way, double-dump, all the way back.

39
00:02:01,781 --> 00:02:03,620
If you run out of ammunition or you're engaged

40
00:02:03,621 --> 00:02:07,220
in a close fight, you may have to employ cold steel, an edged

41
00:02:07,221 --> 00:02:09,760
It's at that point that combat narrows down

42
00:02:09,860 --> 00:02:12,660
to the narrowest perspective, man against man.

43
00:02:12,760 --> 00:02:14,960
And it truly is kill or be killed.

44
00:02:15,060 --> 00:02:16,460
I want to see some good fights.

45
00:02:16,560 --> 00:02:17,360
You understand that?

46
00:02:17,460 --> 00:02:18,360
Yes, sir.

47
00:02:18,460 --> 00:02:19,960
I want to be able to go home and talk about it.

48
00:02:20,060 --> 00:02:21,660
Military combat training has another objective,

49
00:02:21,760 --> 00:02:25,380
forging individual soldiers into a cohesive fighting unit

50
00:02:25,480 --> 00:02:28,880
that will stand together and not break ranks under fire.

51
00:02:28,980 --> 00:02:32,420
But the most important element is learning how to fight to the

52
00:02:32,520 --> 00:02:33,480
death.

53
00:02:33,580 --> 00:02:34,480
You understand that?

54
00:02:34,580 --> 00:02:35,680
Yes, sir.

55
00:02:35,681 --> 00:02:41,440
nothing like the stress of looking face to face with your enemy and physically grappling

56
00:02:41,441 --> 00:02:45,590
with him and killing him. That's the essence of what it is.

57
00:02:45,591 --> 00:02:47,560
You're taking someone's life

58
00:02:47,561 --> 00:02:51,407
deliberately and you can sometimes look in their eye and know who they are.

59
00:02:51,408 --> 00:02:52,108
How do you

60
00:02:52,109 --> 00:02:58,240
train someone for that, to become a killer when all of society's norms say, don't take

61
00:02:58,241 --> 00:02:59,240
life?

62
00:02:59,241 --> 00:03:03,040
Kill! Kill!

63
00:03:03,041 --> 00:03:05,380
This is the first hand-to-hand combat training

64
00:03:05,381 --> 00:03:07,040
these new recruits get.

65
00:03:07,041 --> 00:03:08,340
With the strike!

66
00:03:08,341 --> 00:03:09,041
Strike!

67
00:03:09,042 --> 00:03:10,580
With the strike!

68
00:03:10,581 --> 00:03:13,380
They learn the proper way to take on an opponent.

69
00:03:13,381 --> 00:03:14,780
Elite hand punch, what is it?

70
00:03:14,781 --> 00:03:15,880
Two things.

71
00:03:15,881 --> 00:03:19,080
How to transfer their weight and put power behind a punch.

72
00:03:19,081 --> 00:03:20,700
The moment of impact.

73
00:03:20,701 --> 00:03:22,200
The moment of impact, you understand?

74
00:03:22,201 --> 00:03:24,700
Yes, sir.

75
00:03:24,701 --> 00:03:28,740
35,000 civilians became Marines in 1999,

76
00:03:28,741 --> 00:03:31,740
17,000 of them here at the Marine Corps Recruit Depot

77
00:03:31,741 --> 00:03:36,480
in San Diego, the rest at Parris Island in South Carolina.

78
00:03:36,481 --> 00:03:40,640
The government spent $465 million on marine boot camp

79
00:03:40,641 --> 00:03:43,620
training in 1999.

80
00:03:43,621 --> 00:03:49,660
That works out to about $13,300 per private.

81
00:03:49,661 --> 00:03:54,500
Marine combat training not only takes place on dry land.

82
00:03:54,501 --> 00:03:59,340
By necessity, it also involves the water.

83
00:03:59,341 --> 00:04:02,140
We need to be able to fight while we're wet.

84
00:04:02,240 --> 00:04:04,720
We're teaching Marines how to survive in the water

85
00:04:04,820 --> 00:04:06,480
with all of their combat equipment

86
00:04:06,580 --> 00:04:09,480
so that they can carry the fight onto land.

87
00:04:09,580 --> 00:04:12,280
Some of these recruits are just now learning how to swim.

88
00:04:16,420 --> 00:04:19,000
At this pool, they are taught how to operate in water,

89
00:04:19,100 --> 00:04:20,920
wearing a full load of combat gear

90
00:04:21,020 --> 00:04:25,800
and carrying a seven-pound M16 rifle.

91
00:04:25,900 --> 00:04:28,060
Young recruit standing there, never been in water over his head

92
00:04:28,061 --> 00:04:30,860
first time, loaded down with 45 pounds of equipment,

93
00:04:30,960 --> 00:04:34,860
is told to step off a 3-meter tower into 12 feet of water,

94
00:04:34,960 --> 00:04:35,960
and he does it.

95
00:04:40,400 --> 00:04:42,700
Not everyone is tough enough to make it through this kind

96
00:04:42,800 --> 00:04:44,200
of training.

97
00:04:44,300 --> 00:04:48,120
The boot camp dropout rate is about 11%.

98
00:04:48,220 --> 00:04:50,520
The Marine Corps knows that it is part of a weeding out

99
00:04:50,620 --> 00:04:53,220
process that determines who has what it takes.

100
00:04:53,221 --> 00:04:58,220
We must be prepared to fight, as our hymn says,

101
00:04:58,221 --> 00:05:00,220
in any clime or place.

102
00:05:00,221 --> 00:05:01,220
And we are.

103
00:05:01,221 --> 00:05:02,220
We fought in the desert.

104
00:05:02,221 --> 00:05:03,220
We fought in the jungles.

105
00:05:03,221 --> 00:05:05,220
We fought in cities and towns.

106
00:05:05,221 --> 00:05:08,220
We're ready to fight anywhere.

107
00:05:08,221 --> 00:05:12,220
The challenge to our training is trying to replicate,

108
00:05:12,221 --> 00:05:14,220
to the greatest degree possible,

109
00:05:14,221 --> 00:05:16,220
this battle of the world.

110
00:05:16,221 --> 00:05:18,220
We're ready to fight anywhere.

111
00:05:18,221 --> 00:05:22,220
The challenge to our training is trying to replicate,

112
00:05:22,221 --> 00:05:24,620
possible this diverse environment.

113
00:05:24,720 --> 00:05:29,020
Day, night, hot, cold, wet, dry, doesn't make any difference.

114
00:05:29,120 --> 00:05:32,220
If soldiers have to be ready for every type of terrain,

115
00:05:32,320 --> 00:05:36,360
they also need to be ready for every type of weapon.

116
00:05:36,460 --> 00:05:38,860
Since the beginning of the 20th century,

117
00:05:38,960 --> 00:05:42,980
combat training has included preparing for chemical warfare.

118
00:05:43,080 --> 00:05:46,220
Soldiers in World War I first began learning to put on gas

119
00:05:46,320 --> 00:05:51,020
masks to deal with German nerve gas.

120
00:05:51,021 --> 00:05:57,360
During the 1940s, American GIs continued this kind of training.

121
00:05:57,361 --> 00:05:59,960
The increasing use of chemical weapons today

122
00:05:59,961 --> 00:06:03,080
has made this combat skill an even more important part

123
00:06:03,081 --> 00:06:06,080
of modern marine combat training.

124
00:06:06,081 --> 00:06:08,320
You can't fight in combat blind.

125
00:06:08,321 --> 00:06:10,160
So one thing we try to do is try

126
00:06:10,161 --> 00:06:12,160
to take their attention away from those effects that's

127
00:06:12,161 --> 00:06:13,160
going on with it.

128
00:06:13,161 --> 00:06:15,320
Because if they come into contact with a simple agent

129
00:06:15,321 --> 00:06:16,620
like this, and next thing you know,

130
00:06:16,621 --> 00:06:18,520
they can't perform their job properly,

131
00:06:18,521 --> 00:06:19,780
that's a large problem to us.

132
00:06:19,781 --> 00:06:23,380
At the Camp Pendleton Marine Base in Southern California,

133
00:06:23,480 --> 00:06:26,380
every recruit is subjected to chemical weapons training.

134
00:06:35,660 --> 00:06:39,860
20 men at a time are exposed to CS gas, a nonlethal

135
00:06:39,960 --> 00:06:42,740
but extremely irritating substance.

136
00:06:42,840 --> 00:06:46,280
It stings the skin, the eyes water uncontrollably,

137
00:06:46,281 --> 00:06:49,180
and breathing becomes almost impossible.

138
00:06:49,280 --> 00:06:52,280
This kind of training is more than just instructing the proper

139
00:06:52,380 --> 00:06:55,280
way to use a simple protective apparatus.

140
00:06:55,380 --> 00:06:58,720
It teaches a person the ability to control the urge to panic

141
00:06:58,820 --> 00:06:59,720
under stress.

142
00:07:02,860 --> 00:07:05,540
No army had more discipline and self-control

143
00:07:05,640 --> 00:07:07,940
than that of ancient Rome.

144
00:07:08,040 --> 00:07:11,380
The Roman legions managed to rule the world by brute force,

145
00:07:11,480 --> 00:07:14,400
superb tactics, and a fighting mentality

146
00:07:14,401 --> 00:07:19,400
that had been cultivated during extremely tough training.

147
00:07:19,500 --> 00:07:23,200
It was said of the Roman army that their exercises

148
00:07:23,300 --> 00:07:28,080
were bloodless wars and their wars bloody exercises.

149
00:07:28,180 --> 00:07:30,720
It's because they had the essence of combat training.

150
00:07:30,820 --> 00:07:34,260
They knew that in order to train a soldier to fight as part

151
00:07:34,360 --> 00:07:39,060
of a team against an often valiant, overwhelming enemy,

152
00:07:39,061 --> 00:07:46,820
enemy, they had to teach them with all the realism possible.

153
00:07:46,821 --> 00:07:50,660
Unfortunately most of the images we have of the Roman army and the way in which they may

154
00:07:50,661 --> 00:07:56,060
or may not have trained come from Hollywood spectaculars and the ways in which they portrayed

155
00:07:56,061 --> 00:07:57,060
that.

156
00:07:57,061 --> 00:08:02,160
We don't have a lot of evidence of the specifics of training except that it obviously involved

157
00:08:02,161 --> 00:08:06,160
which was, yes, running, lifting, but also a very mental

158
00:08:06,260 --> 00:08:10,560
component to prepare yourself to work as part of a well-oiled

159
00:08:10,660 --> 00:08:11,700
machine.

160
00:08:11,800 --> 00:08:15,300
The Romans were fanatical about physical fitness.

161
00:08:15,400 --> 00:08:18,340
Their army became legendary for its tireless ability

162
00:08:18,440 --> 00:08:21,520
to march and to fight.

163
00:08:21,620 --> 00:08:23,920
The Romans actually had physical training.

164
00:08:24,020 --> 00:08:25,420
They made them run.

165
00:08:25,520 --> 00:08:27,720
You did not see a fat Roman soldier.

166
00:08:27,820 --> 00:08:29,620
And of course, those soldiers were

167
00:08:29,621 --> 00:08:30,620
Roman soldier.

168
00:08:30,720 --> 00:08:34,060
And of course, those soldiers that didn't seem to be able to

169
00:08:34,160 --> 00:08:38,260
undergo the strict physical training they had would be

170
00:08:38,360 --> 00:08:42,660
punished, literally beaten at times.

171
00:08:42,760 --> 00:08:44,700
The Romans trained for and perfected

172
00:08:44,800 --> 00:08:48,680
the use of the phalanx, a formation of massed infantry

173
00:08:48,780 --> 00:08:51,480
troops bunched tightly together, with their shields

174
00:08:51,580 --> 00:08:55,120
and spears overlapping.

175
00:08:55,220 --> 00:08:57,620
For 1,000 years, their military system

176
00:08:57,621 --> 00:09:00,620
the wonder of the world because the way in which it trained them

177
00:09:00,720 --> 00:09:04,720
as a team to act as part of a machine with total discipline

178
00:09:04,820 --> 00:09:08,100
in realistic environments so that no matter what enemy they

179
00:09:08,200 --> 00:09:12,460
ran against, they had the edge over them.

180
00:09:12,560 --> 00:09:14,640
That emphasis on discipline would also

181
00:09:14,740 --> 00:09:18,140
serve as an ideological example for history's most famous

182
00:09:18,240 --> 00:09:21,440
fighting machine, the Roman gladiator.

183
00:09:21,441 --> 00:09:29,440
A gladiator was a highly skilled, very well-trained risker

184
00:09:29,540 --> 00:09:32,680
of death in a spectacular environment.

185
00:09:35,380 --> 00:09:37,820
Gladiators were mostly slaves, criminals,

186
00:09:37,920 --> 00:09:39,860
or prisoners of war.

187
00:09:39,960 --> 00:09:43,220
They were trained for combat in special privately owned schools.

188
00:09:46,960 --> 00:09:50,640
The gladiatorial schools were, in some sense, prisons,

189
00:09:50,641 --> 00:09:52,780
in some sense, training schools.

190
00:09:52,880 --> 00:09:54,980
The gladiators lived in the school,

191
00:09:55,080 --> 00:09:57,380
and they didn't go outside the school.

192
00:09:57,480 --> 00:09:59,180
So they're sort of self-enclosed areas

193
00:09:59,280 --> 00:10:01,780
where you'd have kitchens, you'd have bath facilities,

194
00:10:01,880 --> 00:10:03,880
you'd have training facilities.

195
00:10:03,980 --> 00:10:06,160
And the training area for a gladiatorial school

196
00:10:06,260 --> 00:10:08,500
was usually an arena.

197
00:10:08,600 --> 00:10:10,800
Gladiators trained under the strict supervision

198
00:10:10,900 --> 00:10:15,800
of expert combat instructors, known as doctores.

199
00:10:15,900 --> 00:10:20,640
The word doctor in Latin simply means one who is learned.

200
00:10:20,641 --> 00:10:24,580
And there were doctores in the gladiatorial school

201
00:10:24,581 --> 00:10:27,880
who were particularly learned in a certain kind of combat,

202
00:10:28,080 --> 00:10:29,480
a certain armature.

203
00:10:29,780 --> 00:10:32,440
And they were primarily responsible for the training

204
00:10:32,441 --> 00:10:35,120
of individual gladiators in a specific armature.

205
00:10:36,820 --> 00:10:38,880
Gladiators trained with weighted shields

206
00:10:38,881 --> 00:10:40,020
to gain strength.

207
00:10:40,780 --> 00:10:43,000
In combat, they wore a visored helmet

208
00:10:43,001 --> 00:10:45,760
and carried a net or perhaps a trident.

209
00:10:46,280 --> 00:10:48,560
But their main weapon was a sword.

210
00:10:48,561 --> 00:10:56,000
One of the clearest elements about the training is the presence of a big stake in the ground,

211
00:10:56,400 --> 00:11:01,980
sort of a practice human, a big log maybe around six feet above the ground,

212
00:11:02,380 --> 00:11:04,620
that was there basically for people to attack.

213
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:08,680
So people would get on their armature and they go attack the log.

214
00:11:09,080 --> 00:11:14,700
And on the basis of the marks left on the log, their trainers could tell where they're letting their guard down,

215
00:11:14,820 --> 00:11:17,240
where they need to watch, where they need to keep their eyes.

216
00:11:17,241 --> 00:11:21,580
So not unlike, perhaps, training for football today,

217
00:11:21,680 --> 00:11:24,840
whamming against something is a key part of the training

218
00:11:24,940 --> 00:11:27,380
of a gladiator, too.

219
00:11:27,480 --> 00:11:29,680
The intense training of both the gladiator

220
00:11:29,780 --> 00:11:33,280
and Roman soldier would set the standard by which future armies

221
00:11:33,380 --> 00:11:34,200
would be measured.

222
00:11:38,100 --> 00:11:41,400
Next, knighthood and teaching the art of killing

223
00:11:41,500 --> 00:11:44,440
during the Middle Ages.

224
00:11:47,240 --> 00:11:54,480
It is a Wednesday night in Houston, Texas.

225
00:11:54,580 --> 00:11:58,960
Members of HACA, the Historical Armed Combat Association,

226
00:11:59,060 --> 00:12:00,780
are holding their weekly meeting

227
00:12:00,880 --> 00:12:02,960
and learning about the medieval fighting

228
00:12:03,060 --> 00:12:03,920
skill of the sword.

229
00:12:04,020 --> 00:12:06,460
OK.

230
00:12:06,560 --> 00:12:10,960
HACA is an association of arms and armor practitioners

231
00:12:11,060 --> 00:12:13,900
and enthusiasts, scholars, researchers,

232
00:12:13,901 --> 00:12:16,840
studying medieval and Renaissance martial art.

233
00:12:16,940 --> 00:12:20,200
We're not practicing it today as they did for killing,

234
00:12:20,300 --> 00:12:23,640
but we can practice it in earnest as a serious martial

235
00:12:23,740 --> 00:12:27,240
art and as a method of self-defense,

236
00:12:27,340 --> 00:12:31,760
not that anyone's going to attack us with a sword today.

237
00:12:31,860 --> 00:12:34,620
Through the centuries, the image of the medieval knight

238
00:12:34,720 --> 00:12:39,560
has been highly romanticized into a figure of chivalry.

239
00:12:39,660 --> 00:12:43,100
But the reality is that these were hardcore soldiers

240
00:12:43,101 --> 00:12:45,600
who had to undergo hardcore training.

241
00:12:49,600 --> 00:12:52,340
The idea was not to deter an opponent, to dissuade him,

242
00:12:52,440 --> 00:12:53,840
to discourage him.

243
00:12:53,940 --> 00:12:56,740
The idea was to kill him.

244
00:12:56,840 --> 00:12:59,380
Medieval warriors trained under the tutelage

245
00:12:59,480 --> 00:13:01,480
of fight masters, who were experts

246
00:13:01,580 --> 00:13:04,220
in the nuances of combat.

247
00:13:04,320 --> 00:13:05,760
Throughout Europe, their students

248
00:13:05,860 --> 00:13:09,460
were both commoners and noblemen.

249
00:13:09,560 --> 00:13:11,460
We know that in the Middle Ages,

250
00:13:11,461 --> 00:13:14,260
starting at least in the 12th century,

251
00:13:14,360 --> 00:13:18,260
that there were fighting guilds and instructors of fencing

252
00:13:18,360 --> 00:13:20,500
who taught various martial arts.

253
00:13:20,600 --> 00:13:22,600
And these martial arts would consist of everything

254
00:13:22,700 --> 00:13:25,140
from the use of staffs and pole weapons

255
00:13:25,240 --> 00:13:29,540
to different types of swords and shields.

256
00:13:29,640 --> 00:13:32,720
From the 13th century to the late 17th century,

257
00:13:32,820 --> 00:13:35,760
these early combat instructors recorded their fighting

258
00:13:35,860 --> 00:13:38,720
techniques in handwritten text and images.

259
00:13:41,460 --> 00:13:45,900
Translated from Latin, this manual gives specific and

260
00:13:46,000 --> 00:13:49,800
detailed instructions in the art of attack.

261
00:13:49,900 --> 00:13:52,700
Thanks to the push I give to your elbow,

262
00:13:52,800 --> 00:13:55,200
I think I'll cut your throat into two halves.

263
00:13:57,300 --> 00:14:00,220
These manuals also showed that medieval warriors

264
00:14:00,320 --> 00:14:04,020
weren't above fighting below the belt.

265
00:14:04,120 --> 00:14:07,160
When I used the body contact with somebody,

266
00:14:07,260 --> 00:14:10,260
I hit his testicles with my right foot.

267
00:14:10,261 --> 00:14:14,060
I'm going to knock you down to the ground

268
00:14:14,160 --> 00:14:16,860
and leave you with no defense so I can hurt you.

269
00:14:22,000 --> 00:14:24,800
During his class, John Clements explains

270
00:14:24,900 --> 00:14:29,920
one of the manual's violently effective takedown techniques.

271
00:14:30,020 --> 00:14:34,120
Yes, here, stopping the energy, coming up again.

272
00:14:34,220 --> 00:14:35,120
He can't cut.

273
00:14:35,220 --> 00:14:36,060
Cut me, please.

274
00:14:36,160 --> 00:14:37,060
Try and slice.

275
00:14:37,160 --> 00:14:38,260
He's not going anywhere.

276
00:14:38,261 --> 00:14:41,700
My body, my leverage, I have leverage, I have balance,

277
00:14:41,800 --> 00:14:44,740
and my weapon is still free to cut him or to hit him

278
00:14:44,840 --> 00:14:46,740
or to disarm him in some way.

279
00:14:46,840 --> 00:14:48,240
Thank you.

280
00:14:48,340 --> 00:14:50,740
The underlying theme in all of this

281
00:14:50,840 --> 00:14:52,940
is brutality and effectiveness.

282
00:14:53,040 --> 00:14:54,140
You weren't playing.

283
00:14:54,240 --> 00:14:55,740
You weren't putting on a show.

284
00:14:55,840 --> 00:14:58,480
You were trying to kill someone and stay alive.

285
00:14:58,580 --> 00:15:01,520
And anything goes under those conditions.

286
00:15:01,620 --> 00:15:04,280
And the manuals tell this pretty clearly.

287
00:15:04,281 --> 00:15:05,180
clearly.

288
00:15:08,180 --> 00:15:10,820
In the Far East, martial arts of a different kind

289
00:15:10,821 --> 00:15:14,020
were developing along more spiritual lines.

290
00:15:14,021 --> 00:15:17,320
For the samurai, a mental state approaching meditation

291
00:15:17,321 --> 00:15:20,120
was essential in combat training.

292
00:15:20,121 --> 00:15:23,140
A Japanese samurai was a warrior for hire,

293
00:15:23,141 --> 00:15:26,460
dedicated to protecting the feudal assets of his master

294
00:15:26,461 --> 00:15:30,800
with unswerving loyalty and dedication.

295
00:15:30,801 --> 00:15:34,140
Typical samurai would probably train six or eight hours a day

296
00:15:34,141 --> 00:15:37,040
And of course, they trained with wooden swords, which

297
00:15:37,140 --> 00:15:38,380
are called bokens.

298
00:15:38,480 --> 00:15:41,380
They trained with real swords.

299
00:15:41,480 --> 00:15:43,880
Samurais would practice individual movements

300
00:15:43,980 --> 00:15:47,380
with their razor sharp katana or sword for hours

301
00:15:47,480 --> 00:15:50,080
and days at a time.

302
00:15:50,180 --> 00:15:53,200
Like the gladiators and knights, the samurai also

303
00:15:53,300 --> 00:15:57,900
trained under the guidance of teachers or masters.

304
00:15:58,000 --> 00:16:02,300
Their incredible repetition also emphasized mental control

305
00:16:02,301 --> 00:16:05,540
and the ability to attain an almost zen-like state

306
00:16:05,640 --> 00:16:07,040
of calmness during battle.

307
00:16:11,200 --> 00:16:15,040
Samurai's training had to be the most realistic possible,

308
00:16:15,140 --> 00:16:20,120
almost more strict and harsher than a real live situation

309
00:16:20,220 --> 00:16:24,060
in combat, because the more they can make it real and face life

310
00:16:24,160 --> 00:16:26,360
and death in their training, when they really face life

311
00:16:26,460 --> 00:16:29,820
or death in an actual match or duel around the battlefield,

312
00:16:29,821 --> 00:16:32,120
it would be very comfortable to them.

313
00:16:32,220 --> 00:16:34,520
It's easy to shoot at targets, but when you have somebody

314
00:16:34,620 --> 00:16:36,020
else that's shooting back at you,

315
00:16:36,120 --> 00:16:38,560
or a boxer that's just hitting a heavy bag when somebody

316
00:16:38,660 --> 00:16:41,460
isn't hitting you back, it adds a completely different level

317
00:16:41,560 --> 00:16:43,760
to that.

318
00:16:43,860 --> 00:16:46,060
Eventually, gunpowder and firearms

319
00:16:46,160 --> 00:16:50,380
would render both the samurai and the knight ineffective.

320
00:16:50,480 --> 00:16:52,880
Improvements in weaponry called for a different type

321
00:16:52,980 --> 00:16:58,080
of training for the new armies of the new era.

322
00:17:00,820 --> 00:17:03,820
In the late 1700s, the North American colonies

323
00:17:03,920 --> 00:17:07,160
of Great Britain were rebelling against the crown.

324
00:17:07,260 --> 00:17:10,460
A new emerging nation was attempting to break away

325
00:17:10,560 --> 00:17:14,860
and form its own democracy on its own terms.

326
00:17:14,960 --> 00:17:17,880
The colonists were willing to fight for their independence,

327
00:17:17,980 --> 00:17:22,480
but they were not properly prepared for battle.

328
00:17:22,580 --> 00:17:25,480
The biggest problem that faced the Americans

329
00:17:25,580 --> 00:17:27,480
at the start of the Revolutionary War

330
00:17:27,481 --> 00:17:32,480
was the fact that there was no single way of doing training.

331
00:17:32,481 --> 00:17:37,480
Each different colony had a different manual

332
00:17:37,481 --> 00:17:39,480
that they used to conduct training.

333
00:17:39,481 --> 00:17:42,480
The training for the individual soldier

334
00:17:42,481 --> 00:17:44,480
was reasonably simple.

335
00:17:44,481 --> 00:17:47,480
I mean, basically all you had to do is teach him how to load

336
00:17:47,481 --> 00:17:53,480
and fire his musket and to operate under stressful situation of combat.

337
00:17:53,481 --> 00:17:56,480
The Continental Army got a much-needed shot in the arm

338
00:17:56,481 --> 00:18:00,720
from an unlikely and unexpected source.

339
00:18:00,820 --> 00:18:05,920
The capstone of American creative combat training

340
00:18:06,020 --> 00:18:09,360
in the American Revolution comes to us in the person

341
00:18:09,460 --> 00:18:14,700
of a fascinating German, Friedrich von Steuben.

342
00:18:14,800 --> 00:18:17,740
And von Steuben, who was not a Baron, who was not von,

343
00:18:17,840 --> 00:18:19,640
who may not even have been an officer,

344
00:18:19,740 --> 00:18:22,000
certainly was never a general, it didn't matter.

345
00:18:22,100 --> 00:18:25,180
When he came to America, he had enough chutzpah,

346
00:18:25,181 --> 00:18:28,080
enough gall to say that he was a Prussian trainer

347
00:18:28,180 --> 00:18:30,420
and that he could whip Washington's army into shape.

348
00:18:30,520 --> 00:18:32,220
The only reason he is such a hero, though,

349
00:18:32,320 --> 00:18:34,520
is he was able to pull it off.

350
00:18:34,620 --> 00:18:37,300
Von Steuben literally wrote the book,

351
00:18:37,400 --> 00:18:40,420
Manual Number One for the US Army.

352
00:18:40,520 --> 00:18:42,840
Its official title was Regulations

353
00:18:42,940 --> 00:18:44,840
for the Order and Discipline of the Troops

354
00:18:44,940 --> 00:18:48,340
of the United States, Part One.

355
00:18:48,440 --> 00:18:50,440
The first part of the book was the school of the soldier,

356
00:18:50,540 --> 00:18:53,940
which taught the individual soldier how to carry his arms,

357
00:18:53,941 --> 00:18:56,840
how to load and fire his musket.

358
00:18:56,940 --> 00:18:59,580
Von Steuben told Washington that although his men

359
00:18:59,680 --> 00:19:02,280
were competent marksmen, they did not know how to use

360
00:19:02,380 --> 00:19:05,080
their bayonets effectively.

361
00:19:05,180 --> 00:19:09,280
In 1778, at their winter camp in Valley Forge, Pennsylvania,

362
00:19:09,380 --> 00:19:13,900
von Steuben began instructing the Continental Army commanders.

363
00:19:14,000 --> 00:19:16,800
He broke down the complicated bayonet techniques that were

364
00:19:16,900 --> 00:19:20,300
practiced in European armies into a series of simple and

365
00:19:20,400 --> 00:19:23,940
numbered movements.

366
00:19:23,941 --> 00:19:27,440
They illustrated precisely how a soldier would grip his musket

367
00:19:27,441 --> 00:19:29,980
and how he would use the weapon in battle.

368
00:19:31,480 --> 00:19:33,840
Von Steuben's numeric instructional methods

369
00:19:33,841 --> 00:19:37,640
gave rise to the phrase, doing it by the numbers.

370
00:19:39,420 --> 00:19:42,820
The officers all agree it's a wonderful system

371
00:19:42,821 --> 00:19:43,880
with one exception.

372
00:19:44,780 --> 00:19:51,060
They need to change the command after you say, ready,

373
00:19:51,061 --> 00:19:56,000
and before you say fire, to aim.

374
00:19:57,300 --> 00:19:59,380
Steuben had done, like the Europeans,

375
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:01,920
made it present your fire locks,

376
00:20:02,720 --> 00:20:06,200
which means point in the general direction of the enemy.

377
00:20:06,760 --> 00:20:08,380
The Americans say, no, no,

378
00:20:08,940 --> 00:20:11,320
even though it's not very accurate,

379
00:20:11,321 --> 00:20:16,140
we really do think we ought to point it at some specific guy.

380
00:20:18,400 --> 00:20:20,540
Von Steuben made another strategic decision.

381
00:20:21,060 --> 00:20:24,060
moving the army off their flat parade grounds

382
00:20:24,061 --> 00:20:28,060
and training in the rugged territory nearby.

383
00:20:30,060 --> 00:20:34,060
In my opinion, Steuben's most important contribution

384
00:20:34,061 --> 00:20:40,060
to the American army's philosophy of realistic combat training

385
00:20:40,061 --> 00:20:45,060
is the introduction of the notion that you can only train

386
00:20:45,061 --> 00:20:49,060
in the broken ground

387
00:20:49,061 --> 00:20:52,960
the kinds of areas where you really would expect to fight.

388
00:20:53,060 --> 00:20:57,100
This is the essence of what, in the late 20th century,

389
00:20:57,200 --> 00:21:03,540
came to be called train as you fight, fight as you train.

390
00:21:03,640 --> 00:21:06,780
Under von Steuben's tutelage, the American army evolved

391
00:21:06,880 --> 00:21:10,840
from a ragtag bunch of militia in 1775

392
00:21:10,940 --> 00:21:14,020
to an organized fighting unit.

393
00:21:14,120 --> 00:21:16,880
By the Battle of Yorktown in 1781,

394
00:21:16,881 --> 00:21:21,120
They were equal to any of the professional armies of Europe.

395
00:21:21,220 --> 00:21:23,480
With von Steuben's leadership, the colonies

396
00:21:23,580 --> 00:21:25,920
rid themselves of the British and became

397
00:21:26,020 --> 00:21:27,820
an independent nation.

398
00:21:27,920 --> 00:21:31,720
Von Steuben became an American citizen in 1783.

399
00:21:31,820 --> 00:21:34,340
A statue in his honor stands across the street

400
00:21:34,440 --> 00:21:35,340
from the White House.

401
00:21:41,440 --> 00:21:44,640
In 1802, the military academy at West Point

402
00:21:44,641 --> 00:21:48,640
was created to teach combat strategy to army officers.

403
00:21:48,740 --> 00:21:52,440
By the beginning of the Mexican-American War in 1846,

404
00:21:52,540 --> 00:21:55,380
the army was still small by modern standards.

405
00:21:55,480 --> 00:21:59,420
Only about 9,000 professional soldiers were under arms.

406
00:21:59,520 --> 00:22:02,120
But most were foot soldiers or cavalry troops

407
00:22:02,220 --> 00:22:04,520
who had practical experience fighting Indians

408
00:22:04,620 --> 00:22:07,020
on the frontier.

409
00:22:07,120 --> 00:22:10,240
Their primary training was in marching and drilling,

410
00:22:10,340 --> 00:22:14,640
and they were learning with new breech-loading rifles.

411
00:22:14,641 --> 00:22:19,640
At the time, Abraham Lincoln was a young congressman from Illinois.

412
00:22:19,641 --> 00:22:25,640
Less than two decades later, he would lead the country during the Civil War.

413
00:22:25,641 --> 00:22:31,640
There was combat training in the Civil War, but there was no standardization in that combat training.

414
00:22:31,641 --> 00:22:37,640
Very little troops, because of the national emergency, had the time to actually be in a camp of instruction

415
00:22:37,641 --> 00:22:39,794
where they were trained in combat methods.

416
00:22:39,795 --> 00:22:43,640
The result would be that the actual combat training would be combat itself.

417
00:22:43,641 --> 00:22:47,940
itself. In the 1860s, the country

418
00:22:47,941 --> 00:22:51,580
was ill-prepared for any war, much less a civil war.

419
00:22:51,581 --> 00:22:55,060
Most of the Union army was made up of volunteer units.

420
00:22:55,061 --> 00:22:57,180
Their officers were ill-equipped to lead.

421
00:23:00,220 --> 00:23:03,200
Most of these officers, weeks before the unit is organized,

422
00:23:03,201 --> 00:23:06,660
were bankers, merchants, lawyers.

423
00:23:06,661 --> 00:23:08,700
Most had no prior military experience.

424
00:23:08,701 --> 00:23:12,540
So it was basically ignorance on their part.

425
00:23:12,541 --> 00:23:14,500
The Civil War soldier's main weapon

426
00:23:14,501 --> 00:23:17,580
had been dramatically improved by adding grooved spirals

427
00:23:17,581 --> 00:23:20,580
inside the rifle's barrel.

428
00:23:20,581 --> 00:23:23,040
It increased both range and accuracy.

429
00:23:25,780 --> 00:23:29,220
And the Army is basing its strategy and tactics

430
00:23:29,221 --> 00:23:30,800
on the 18th century.

431
00:23:30,801 --> 00:23:34,500
And suddenly, you have a weapon that is extremely accurate,

432
00:23:34,501 --> 00:23:40,200
out to 200 to 500 yards, has a lethal range of 1,000 yards.

433
00:23:40,201 --> 00:23:42,240
And you're still using these 18th century tactics

434
00:23:42,241 --> 00:23:46,080
shoulder to shoulder, firing into an enemy position 50,

435
00:23:46,180 --> 00:23:50,180
100 yards away, it's going to increase casualties.

436
00:23:52,120 --> 00:23:53,780
While it would have made more sense

437
00:23:53,781 --> 00:23:56,380
from a tactical standpoint to spread out the troops

438
00:23:56,381 --> 00:23:59,360
and make them less of a target, the soldiers needed

439
00:23:59,361 --> 00:24:01,620
to be close together to hear their commanders.

440
00:24:02,700 --> 00:24:05,000
Moving them apart would have lost the key element

441
00:24:05,001 --> 00:24:06,260
of command and control.

442
00:24:07,540 --> 00:24:10,400
That's also why you had to train them to stand

443
00:24:10,401 --> 00:24:14,440
face of withering fire, even when in some cases 40 or 50%

444
00:24:14,540 --> 00:24:17,400
of your comrades had just died the minute before.

445
00:24:17,500 --> 00:24:20,880
You had to still stand there and try to deliver that aimed

446
00:24:20,980 --> 00:24:22,820
volley fire.

447
00:24:22,920 --> 00:24:25,240
It is not possible to teach the raw courage

448
00:24:25,340 --> 00:24:28,780
necessary to face such an onslaught of gunfire.

449
00:24:28,880 --> 00:24:31,820
Military leaders believe the only way to train soldiers

450
00:24:31,920 --> 00:24:33,860
for the intensity of the battlefield

451
00:24:33,960 --> 00:24:35,520
was through the constant repetition

452
00:24:35,620 --> 00:24:38,460
of drilling with rifles.

453
00:24:38,461 --> 00:24:41,460
The goal was that soldiers would develop the almost robotic

454
00:24:41,560 --> 00:24:43,460
instincts necessary to survive.

455
00:24:51,600 --> 00:24:53,800
It was the closest thing to hell on Earth

456
00:24:53,900 --> 00:24:56,420
that mankind had ever seen.

457
00:24:56,520 --> 00:24:58,040
Combat during the First World War

458
00:24:58,140 --> 00:25:03,020
was unspeakably brutal and emotionally devastating.

459
00:25:03,120 --> 00:25:06,220
World War I, what was the nature of the combat?

460
00:25:06,221 --> 00:25:10,220
Well, it was large masses of men, perhaps in trenches,

461
00:25:10,320 --> 00:25:14,160
fighting at fairly close range with masses of supporting arms.

462
00:25:14,260 --> 00:25:15,160
The training reflected that.

463
00:25:18,060 --> 00:25:21,060
Soldiers in the early 1900s had one basic weapon

464
00:25:21,160 --> 00:25:24,680
at their disposal, a rifle with a bayonet.

465
00:25:24,780 --> 00:25:28,580
Americans practiced in small groups at close range.

466
00:25:28,680 --> 00:25:31,720
British soldiers who had already gone over the top in battle

467
00:25:31,820 --> 00:25:36,220
and survived the trench warfare became their teachers.

468
00:25:36,221 --> 00:25:39,320
The recruits honed their reflexes by facing away

469
00:25:39,420 --> 00:25:41,820
from their instructors, then suddenly turning

470
00:25:41,920 --> 00:25:44,460
and thrusting their bayonets at a small loop on the end

471
00:25:44,560 --> 00:25:47,060
of a long stick.

472
00:25:47,160 --> 00:25:48,760
This helped them perfect their aim.

473
00:25:52,980 --> 00:25:55,880
Unlike their counterparts in the Revolutionary War,

474
00:25:55,980 --> 00:25:58,740
these soldiers were schooled in how to carry their weapon

475
00:25:58,840 --> 00:26:01,180
through simulated trench warfare conditions.

476
00:26:06,220 --> 00:26:11,400
Huge open fields were converted into gigantic mock battlefields.

477
00:26:11,401 --> 00:26:13,600
Doughboys ran across hundreds of yards

478
00:26:13,601 --> 00:26:16,800
of intricate obstacle courses, complete with rows

479
00:26:16,801 --> 00:26:20,240
of bayonet target dummies, as well as dozens of trenches,

480
00:26:20,241 --> 00:26:24,200
hills, and walls.

481
00:26:24,201 --> 00:26:28,540
They were being trained in the physical tools of combat.

482
00:26:28,541 --> 00:26:30,580
Unfortunately, there was no way to train

483
00:26:30,581 --> 00:26:33,320
for the devastating emotional and psychological effects

484
00:26:33,321 --> 00:26:35,540
of the First World War.

485
00:26:35,541 --> 00:26:40,040
as it was called, claimed thousands of casualties.

486
00:26:40,140 --> 00:26:42,040
Training did take place, however,

487
00:26:42,140 --> 00:26:45,080
as part of the recovery process.

488
00:26:45,180 --> 00:26:48,180
Many shell shock victims were evacuated to hospitals,

489
00:26:48,280 --> 00:26:50,080
where drilling and marching continued

490
00:26:50,180 --> 00:26:53,300
in order to reinforce the idea that they were soldiers

491
00:26:53,400 --> 00:26:55,800
and not casualties.

492
00:26:55,900 --> 00:26:58,260
The Army tried to prepare its troops for the horror

493
00:26:58,360 --> 00:27:01,060
of battle by making its basic combat training

494
00:27:01,160 --> 00:27:03,840
as realistic as possible.

495
00:27:03,841 --> 00:27:09,300
But in World War I, as in all wars, the training could only go so far.

496
00:27:10,840 --> 00:27:13,840
No matter how realistic you try to make the training,

497
00:27:14,220 --> 00:27:18,820
even if you recreated the set of Saving Private Ryan for each one of your platoons

498
00:27:18,821 --> 00:27:24,620
and ran them through the simulated fire or had occasional live bullets fired over their heads,

499
00:27:25,100 --> 00:27:28,220
the soldier will always think to himself during the training,

500
00:27:28,420 --> 00:27:31,900
well, this is still just training. Those shells aren't really out to get me.

501
00:27:31,901 --> 00:27:34,260
It will get his adrenaline flowing, focus his mind so

502
00:27:34,360 --> 00:27:36,940
that mentally he becomes better prepared.

503
00:27:37,040 --> 00:27:38,040
But truly preparing?

504
00:27:41,840 --> 00:27:45,340
Stronger soldiers have always made better fighters.

505
00:27:45,440 --> 00:27:48,380
By World War I, the military had begun to stress a greater

506
00:27:48,480 --> 00:27:51,280
amount of exercise and increased physical fitness

507
00:27:51,380 --> 00:27:54,620
during combat training.

508
00:27:54,720 --> 00:27:59,320
Army workouts can be traced to the gymnasium at West Point.

509
00:27:59,321 --> 00:28:03,020
During the early 1900s, cadets worked out with rings suspended

510
00:28:03,120 --> 00:28:05,660
from the ceiling.

511
00:28:05,760 --> 00:28:11,460
They also boxed and wrestled.

512
00:28:11,560 --> 00:28:15,560
For army troops, the 1914 Manual of Military Training

513
00:28:15,660 --> 00:28:18,780
recommended there be five 45-minute long workout

514
00:28:18,880 --> 00:28:19,680
periods a week.

515
00:28:24,320 --> 00:28:27,520
Many of the drills focused on agility training.

516
00:28:27,521 --> 00:28:29,760
It was thought that these kinds of exercises

517
00:28:29,860 --> 00:28:31,720
would help prepare a soldier to navigate

518
00:28:31,820 --> 00:28:35,360
the uncertain terrain of the battlefield.

519
00:28:35,460 --> 00:28:37,360
A primitive form of resistance training,

520
00:28:37,460 --> 00:28:39,740
such as lifting rocks, also helped create

521
00:28:39,840 --> 00:28:40,860
a more physical soldier.

522
00:28:46,740 --> 00:28:50,380
Unfortunately, the lessons of World War I were soon forgotten.

523
00:28:54,020 --> 00:28:56,320
There was no attempt to train for the next war,

524
00:28:56,321 --> 00:28:59,920
Because after all, World War I was the war to end all wars.

525
00:29:00,020 --> 00:29:01,320
So why bother?

526
00:29:01,420 --> 00:29:02,920
We cut the training back.

527
00:29:03,020 --> 00:29:08,660
We cut back on training dollars, training equipment, ammunition.

528
00:29:08,760 --> 00:29:10,860
Germany did the opposite.

529
00:29:10,960 --> 00:29:13,880
Beaten and humiliated after World War I,

530
00:29:13,980 --> 00:29:18,280
it covertly militarized during the 1920s and 30s.

531
00:29:18,380 --> 00:29:23,820
German troops secretly trained with the Red Army in Russia.

532
00:29:23,821 --> 00:29:27,460
They would be known as the Black Reichswehr, or Hidden Army.

533
00:29:30,760 --> 00:29:33,220
The almost fanatical training of Nazi soldiers

534
00:29:33,320 --> 00:29:37,660
mirrored Germany's patriotic fervor of the times.

535
00:29:37,760 --> 00:29:40,360
They were trained extensively in small units,

536
00:29:40,460 --> 00:29:42,680
with the express purpose of establishing

537
00:29:42,780 --> 00:29:44,480
a unique esprit de corps.

538
00:29:49,980 --> 00:29:52,220
At the beginning of the Second World War,

539
00:29:52,221 --> 00:29:55,160
the United States turned to the Selective Service System

540
00:29:55,260 --> 00:29:59,160
to rapidly replenish its military ranks.

541
00:29:59,260 --> 00:30:01,460
During boot camp, the military stressed

542
00:30:01,560 --> 00:30:04,560
basic physical fitness rather than combat stamina.

543
00:30:08,460 --> 00:30:11,080
We had a more scientific appreciation

544
00:30:11,180 --> 00:30:13,780
of the value of exercise and sport

545
00:30:13,880 --> 00:30:15,580
and cardiovascular conditioning.

546
00:30:15,680 --> 00:30:18,020
The basic training was that averaged out

547
00:30:18,120 --> 00:30:21,420
to about half an hour of calisthenics a day.

548
00:30:21,421 --> 00:30:24,520
But for the World War II generation,

549
00:30:24,620 --> 00:30:28,620
a half hour of calisthenics a day was an impressive departure

550
00:30:28,720 --> 00:30:31,760
from their previous attention to calisthenics

551
00:30:31,860 --> 00:30:34,860
and sport-like activities.

552
00:30:34,960 --> 00:30:36,660
Finding the proper equipment to train

553
00:30:36,760 --> 00:30:39,280
the massive influx of new soldiers for combat

554
00:30:39,380 --> 00:30:40,580
was difficult at first.

555
00:30:43,880 --> 00:30:46,620
Still, in 1941, even after we went to war,

556
00:30:46,720 --> 00:30:50,280
you had soldiers using small bags of flour as hand grenades.

557
00:30:50,281 --> 00:30:54,680
You had people using pieces of wood as simulated anti-tank

558
00:30:54,780 --> 00:30:57,880
weapons, tanks made out of cardboard or plywood.

559
00:30:57,980 --> 00:31:02,900
You still didn't have what you needed to train realistically.

560
00:31:03,000 --> 00:31:05,760
In fact, early shortages were so severe

561
00:31:05,860 --> 00:31:09,240
that the army was forced to use empty beer cans as make-believe

562
00:31:09,340 --> 00:31:10,060
mortar shells.

563
00:31:13,300 --> 00:31:15,040
While the US was desperately trying

564
00:31:15,140 --> 00:31:18,140
to train hundreds of thousands of new soldiers,

565
00:31:18,141 --> 00:31:20,780
It recognized the need to train some elite troops

566
00:31:20,880 --> 00:31:25,320
in special combat techniques, techniques that routinely

567
00:31:25,420 --> 00:31:27,420
went above and beyond the call of duty.

568
00:31:30,520 --> 00:31:33,560
For the Army, it's the Rangers.

569
00:31:33,660 --> 00:31:37,660
The Rangers originated during the World War II period

570
00:31:37,760 --> 00:31:41,960
as a specialized unit adapted on the model of the British

571
00:31:42,060 --> 00:31:47,600
commandos, who were intended to be a strategic asset that

572
00:31:47,601 --> 00:31:50,260
that was used for tactical purposes.

573
00:31:53,200 --> 00:31:57,040
Marine training is as tough as it gets for regular troops.

574
00:31:57,140 --> 00:32:01,740
But America's elite warriors are pushed to a higher standard.

575
00:32:01,840 --> 00:32:04,660
Since World War II, all of the branches of the military

576
00:32:04,760 --> 00:32:07,760
have developed special operations forces.

577
00:32:07,860 --> 00:32:09,920
In addition to the Rangers, the Army

578
00:32:10,020 --> 00:32:12,960
had the Green Berets and currently trains the Delta

579
00:32:13,060 --> 00:32:13,860
Force.

580
00:32:17,600 --> 00:32:20,840
For the Navy, it's the SEALS, whose acronym

581
00:32:20,940 --> 00:32:24,240
stands for Sea, Air, and Land.

582
00:32:24,340 --> 00:32:26,340
Training of such elite fighting units

583
00:32:26,440 --> 00:32:28,340
is both constant and grueling.

584
00:32:31,540 --> 00:32:38,660
They specialize in guerrilla warfare, hostage rescue,

585
00:32:38,760 --> 00:32:42,160
and anti-terrorism tactics.

586
00:32:42,260 --> 00:32:44,960
All of their training prepares them for combat.

587
00:32:47,600 --> 00:32:49,640
in almost any environment.

588
00:32:49,740 --> 00:32:52,240
The ranger school, scuba school, visual tracking school,

589
00:32:52,340 --> 00:32:54,640
mountaineering school, outweather survival school,

590
00:32:54,740 --> 00:32:57,840
you're probably going at least 18 hours a day.

591
00:32:57,940 --> 00:33:00,340
And you're getting up 3, 3.30 in the morning.

592
00:33:00,440 --> 00:33:01,840
You're running 10 miles.

593
00:33:01,940 --> 00:33:04,580
Then you're doing patrols through the whole day,

594
00:33:04,680 --> 00:33:08,520
small unit tactics, guerrilla warfare, basically,

595
00:33:08,620 --> 00:33:11,920
and every type of scenario possible until late at night.

596
00:33:11,921 --> 00:33:19,420
During the Vietnam War, battles were fought in swamps

597
00:33:19,520 --> 00:33:20,420
and jungles.

598
00:33:22,600 --> 00:33:24,660
In the Persian Gulf, soldiers fought

599
00:33:24,760 --> 00:33:26,360
in dusty desert conditions.

600
00:33:29,480 --> 00:33:32,980
The consensus today is that future combat troops will need

601
00:33:33,080 --> 00:33:35,380
to know how to fight in an urban environment.

602
00:33:41,920 --> 00:33:43,920
The world's population lives in cities.

603
00:33:43,921 --> 00:33:48,460
If you look back in history, the major fights that have occurred in urban areas have been

604
00:33:48,461 --> 00:33:51,220
incredibly expensive and violent.

605
00:33:51,221 --> 00:33:54,160
Huey City, Stalingrad, Berlin.

606
00:33:54,161 --> 00:33:58,280
It's difficult to distinguish friend from foe, and the engagements are almost always

607
00:33:58,281 --> 00:33:59,280
at very close range.

608
00:34:11,920 --> 00:34:16,360
At Camp Lejeune in North Carolina,

609
00:34:16,460 --> 00:34:19,160
a unique facility gives Marines a chance

610
00:34:19,260 --> 00:34:21,260
to train for firefights on city streets.

611
00:34:32,280 --> 00:34:35,440
This is called M.O.T. training, an acronym

612
00:34:35,540 --> 00:34:38,540
which stands for Military Operations on Urban Terrain.

613
00:34:41,920 --> 00:34:43,580
The holy grail for combat training

614
00:34:43,680 --> 00:34:47,420
is to try to simulate combat to the greatest degree possible.

615
00:34:47,520 --> 00:34:49,800
The nature of combat in urban terrain

616
00:34:49,900 --> 00:34:52,800
places special demands on the techniques and the tactics

617
00:34:52,900 --> 00:34:54,640
and procedures that you will employ there.

618
00:34:59,040 --> 00:35:02,500
The confusion, the smoke, the rubble, the uncertainty

619
00:35:02,600 --> 00:35:04,640
are also magnified in urban areas.

620
00:35:11,920 --> 00:35:15,060
At this training facility, a computer system

621
00:35:15,160 --> 00:35:17,560
tracks Marines with a grid-like network

622
00:35:17,660 --> 00:35:20,660
of infrared transmitters and ultrasonic receivers.

623
00:35:23,760 --> 00:35:25,660
A global positioning satellite follows

624
00:35:25,760 --> 00:35:27,960
each Marine's movements.

625
00:35:28,060 --> 00:35:31,180
Each electronic shot they fire with a specially equipped

626
00:35:31,280 --> 00:35:35,280
M16 rifle, or each electronic hand grenade they throw,

627
00:35:35,380 --> 00:35:37,480
is monitored.

628
00:35:37,580 --> 00:35:41,080
The data is beamed by microwave to a nearby combat training

629
00:35:41,081 --> 00:35:43,980
Training Operations Center, where every detail of the

630
00:35:44,080 --> 00:35:47,320
exercise is recorded and evaluated with computer

631
00:35:47,420 --> 00:35:49,320
generated graphic animation.

632
00:36:02,040 --> 00:36:05,240
21st century combat warriors are training for future

633
00:36:05,340 --> 00:36:07,840
battles with the use of sophisticated weapons

634
00:36:07,940 --> 00:36:08,740
technology.

635
00:36:11,080 --> 00:36:17,020
In 1997, Cubic Defense Systems, a San Diego electronics company,

636
00:36:17,460 --> 00:36:20,680
introduced new technology to upgrade the laser-based Miles

637
00:36:20,681 --> 00:36:23,720
System, first introduced in the late 1970s.

638
00:36:24,560 --> 00:36:28,140
In the multiple integrated laser engagement system known as Miles

639
00:36:28,141 --> 00:36:31,720
2000, infrared impulses replace bullets.

640
00:36:36,360 --> 00:36:40,560
During combat training, soldiers wear vests that are equipped with

641
00:36:40,561 --> 00:36:42,060
detection devices.

642
00:36:45,560 --> 00:36:49,060
Sensors record each soldier's electronic player identification

643
00:36:49,061 --> 00:36:50,060
number.

644
00:36:50,061 --> 00:36:53,060
And when they are hit by the pulse of the electronic bullet fired

645
00:36:53,061 --> 00:36:57,060
by a Miles-equipped rifle, the system records and computes

646
00:36:57,061 --> 00:36:59,060
the casualty status of the hit.

647
00:37:03,060 --> 00:37:07,060
The guns are loaded with blanks to make the training as realistic

648
00:37:07,061 --> 00:37:08,060
as possible.

649
00:37:10,560 --> 00:37:13,400
Now you know if you've been hit or wounded

650
00:37:13,500 --> 00:37:15,200
or if someone's shooting at you,

651
00:37:15,300 --> 00:37:18,100
and it allows you to tailor your training

652
00:37:18,200 --> 00:37:20,900
to a simulated combat environment.

653
00:37:21,000 --> 00:37:23,200
It's a wonderful system.

654
00:37:23,300 --> 00:37:25,000
With the laser engagement systems,

655
00:37:25,100 --> 00:37:27,620
when you're out of ammunition, the laser doesn't work.

656
00:37:32,520 --> 00:37:35,660
Miles' gear can also be mounted on a tank's cannon,

657
00:37:35,760 --> 00:37:38,220
as well as various other mobile weapons,

658
00:37:38,221 --> 00:37:41,520
allowing for full combat simulations involving entire

659
00:37:41,620 --> 00:37:42,620
tank battalions.

660
00:37:51,060 --> 00:37:53,260
Special pyrotechnic charges are fired

661
00:37:53,360 --> 00:37:56,180
to make these battles look and sound like the real thing.

662
00:37:59,280 --> 00:38:02,520
Thanks to modern technology, before tank crews train

663
00:38:02,620 --> 00:38:04,380
and field exercises, they can now

664
00:38:04,480 --> 00:38:08,220
get combat experience on a virtual battlefield.

665
00:38:08,221 --> 00:38:15,220
These modules are detailed replicas of real tanks.

666
00:38:15,320 --> 00:38:19,060
They are called close combat tactical trainers,

667
00:38:19,160 --> 00:38:21,560
and their computer-generated animation programs

668
00:38:21,660 --> 00:38:24,460
can be changed to create any kind of virtual battle

669
00:38:24,560 --> 00:38:25,480
scenario.

670
00:38:25,580 --> 00:38:29,280
Computer hookups allow additional personnel

671
00:38:29,380 --> 00:38:32,280
from different military bases around the country

672
00:38:32,380 --> 00:38:34,580
to take part in the training process.

673
00:38:34,581 --> 00:38:37,880
bases around the country to take part in the training

674
00:38:37,980 --> 00:38:43,020
simultaneously, sharing the same virtual terrain databases.

675
00:38:43,120 --> 00:38:44,820
The soldier of the future will need

676
00:38:44,920 --> 00:38:49,320
to be a highly qualified and capable individual who

677
00:38:49,420 --> 00:38:55,440
is able to use modern technology adeptly.

678
00:38:55,540 --> 00:38:57,940
Technology has truly revolutionized combat

679
00:38:58,040 --> 00:38:59,840
training.

680
00:38:59,940 --> 00:39:04,580
However, some wonder if it's too much of a good thing.

681
00:39:04,581 --> 00:39:07,480
How much should we emphasize technology?

682
00:39:07,580 --> 00:39:11,380
Do we put all our eggs in the technology basket

683
00:39:11,480 --> 00:39:16,960
and give soldiers all sorts of equipment and methods

684
00:39:17,060 --> 00:39:20,500
to utilize this technology, and suddenly

685
00:39:20,600 --> 00:39:22,660
the technology breaks down?

686
00:39:22,760 --> 00:39:25,300
Is the soldier equipped enough to then

687
00:39:25,400 --> 00:39:28,800
go to the old-fashioned methods of fighting war?

688
00:39:32,300 --> 00:39:33,880
Partially because of that concern,

689
00:39:33,881 --> 00:39:38,120
In 1996, the Marine Corps added what they call the Crucible

690
00:39:38,220 --> 00:39:41,480
to the end of the 13-week boot camp.

691
00:39:41,580 --> 00:39:46,920
It's part final exam, part military rite of passage.

692
00:39:47,020 --> 00:39:49,600
The Crucible begins at 2 o'clock in the morning

693
00:39:49,700 --> 00:39:52,760
and continues nonstop for 2 and 1 half days.

694
00:39:52,860 --> 00:39:56,000
The Crucible is the culminating event

695
00:39:56,100 --> 00:39:57,500
in Marine recruit training.

696
00:39:57,600 --> 00:40:00,080
It is the defining moment of the recruits' experience

697
00:40:00,180 --> 00:40:01,540
here in boot camp.

698
00:40:03,880 --> 00:40:07,120
Marine boot camp was already plenty tough,

699
00:40:07,220 --> 00:40:10,980
but combat training teaches individual fighting skills.

700
00:40:11,080 --> 00:40:14,120
The Crucible was designed to emphasize teamwork.

701
00:40:19,220 --> 00:40:23,040
Combat is incredibly chaotic, very confusing,

702
00:40:23,140 --> 00:40:27,040
physically demanding, stressful in the extreme.

703
00:40:27,140 --> 00:40:30,540
We can replicate some of those things in the Crucible.

704
00:40:30,541 --> 00:40:31,540
in the crucible.

705
00:40:31,541 --> 00:40:32,540
Stepping up right now!

706
00:40:32,541 --> 00:40:33,540
I'm stepping up right now!

707
00:40:33,541 --> 00:40:38,600
There's some 3,100 square acres here, and they're going to cover 40 miles on their feet

708
00:40:38,601 --> 00:40:41,420
by the time they're done in these two and a half days.

709
00:40:41,421 --> 00:40:45,780
We're asking them to do things that they may not have thought were possible before.

710
00:40:45,781 --> 00:40:54,660
I'm asking them to push themselves towards their limits.

711
00:40:54,661 --> 00:40:59,660
Over the next 54 hours, these recruits will work

712
00:40:59,760 --> 00:41:01,400
and they will sweat.

713
00:41:01,500 --> 00:41:04,100
Outfitted with a full combat load of equipment,

714
00:41:04,200 --> 00:41:07,240
they will march from rifle range to obstacle course,

715
00:41:07,340 --> 00:41:10,780
never resting for more than a few minutes at a time.

716
00:41:10,880 --> 00:41:14,320
A key element of the crucible is sleep deprivation.

717
00:41:14,420 --> 00:41:17,320
These recruits are only allowed eight hours of shut eye

718
00:41:17,420 --> 00:41:20,680
during the two-day period.

719
00:41:20,780 --> 00:41:22,980
The crucible is long on training,

720
00:41:22,981 --> 00:41:26,480
training, but short on rations.

721
00:41:26,580 --> 00:41:28,980
Recruits are allowed to drink plenty of water,

722
00:41:29,080 --> 00:41:33,120
but they're given only two small MREs, the military term

723
00:41:33,220 --> 00:41:36,520
for meals ready to eat.

724
00:41:36,620 --> 00:41:40,440
Get your muscle out of the dirt.

725
00:41:40,540 --> 00:41:43,540
In this scenario, a squad is given 25 minutes

726
00:41:43,640 --> 00:41:46,980
to get through an obstacle course laced with barbed wire

727
00:41:47,080 --> 00:41:51,080
while carrying their weapons and loads of heavy equipment.

728
00:41:51,180 --> 00:41:51,980
Hello, you.

729
00:41:51,981 --> 00:41:56,480
That's not the only lane.

730
00:41:56,580 --> 00:41:57,320
Let's go.

731
00:41:57,420 --> 00:41:58,420
Go around him.

732
00:41:58,520 --> 00:42:00,420
If you're in a combat environment, especially when

733
00:42:00,520 --> 00:42:02,420
they get to the wire up there, we want to show them

734
00:42:02,520 --> 00:42:04,520
that whenever you move from obstacle to obstacle,

735
00:42:04,620 --> 00:42:07,720
you want to try to keep a low silhouette as possible.

736
00:42:07,820 --> 00:42:08,740
Hello.

737
00:42:08,840 --> 00:42:10,940
One man is designated as a casualty.

738
00:42:11,040 --> 00:42:12,040
Let's go.

739
00:42:12,140 --> 00:42:12,940
Hello.

740
00:42:13,040 --> 00:42:16,440
Others must drag or carry him to the end of the course.

741
00:42:16,540 --> 00:42:18,940
Are you going to let him lay there and plead to death,

742
00:42:19,040 --> 00:42:20,740
or are you going to get him aboard the halo?

743
00:42:20,741 --> 00:42:23,540
At the end of the crucible, these men

744
00:42:23,640 --> 00:42:25,680
will officially become Marines.

745
00:42:25,780 --> 00:42:27,780
They will have pushed themselves to the limit,

746
00:42:27,880 --> 00:42:30,620
enduring the high stress of simulated combat,

747
00:42:30,720 --> 00:42:34,280
along with a physical challenge of food and sleep deprivation.

748
00:42:34,380 --> 00:42:36,060
During their military careers, they

749
00:42:36,160 --> 00:42:39,120
will receive more specialized combat training,

750
00:42:39,220 --> 00:42:43,100
preparing for the reality beyond all training.

751
00:42:43,200 --> 00:42:44,600
The nature of war is immutable.

752
00:42:44,700 --> 00:42:46,540
The fabric of war has not changed

753
00:42:46,640 --> 00:42:50,740
since the first two cavemen tried to beat each other with sticks.

754
00:42:50,741 --> 00:42:54,740
It's a combination of fear, uncertainty, the desire to impose

755
00:42:54,840 --> 00:42:59,480
your will on another, violence, directed or undirected violence.

756
00:42:59,580 --> 00:43:01,980
All these things remain today.

757
00:43:04,080 --> 00:43:07,500
During the Civil War, Union General William Tecumseh

758
00:43:07,600 --> 00:43:10,400
Sherman said, war is cruelty.

759
00:43:10,500 --> 00:43:12,400
There is no use trying to reform it.

760
00:43:12,500 --> 00:43:16,400
The crueler it is, the sooner it will be over.

761
00:43:16,500 --> 00:43:19,040
Across the centuries, the hellish essence of war

762
00:43:19,041 --> 00:43:21,840
of war has remained unchanged.

763
00:43:21,940 --> 00:43:25,240
On the battlefield, it has always been man versus man,

764
00:43:25,340 --> 00:43:28,040
kill or be killed.

765
00:43:28,140 --> 00:43:32,080
Nevertheless, if combat is an evil element of human nature,

766
00:43:32,180 --> 00:43:36,600
then training for it will always be a most necessary evil.

